#!/usr/bin/env python3
#  VELVET  ·  bin/velvet-livewall
#  Live wallpapers: a picture that moves — leaves that fall, stars that
#  twinkle, mist that drifts — made from the still itself, as a seamless loop
#  the wallpaper plays (and a GIF to keep or share). Velly makes one for every
#  favourite by herself (services/LiveWalls.qml runs the queue); this is the
#  workshop. numpy + PIL + ffmpeg.
#
#    velvet-livewall make FILE [options]   make (or remake) the live version
#    velvet-livewall analyse FILE          what Velly reads in the picture, and the style she picks
#    velvet-livewall list                  every live wallpaper made (JSON with --json)
#    velvet-livewall favourites [options]  make what the favourites are missing, one after another
#    velvet-livewall remove FILE|all       delete live versions
#    velvet-livewall restore FILE          the version before the last remake back (again: forward)
#    velvet-livewall understand "TEXT"     how a description is read (JSON)
#    velvet-livewall --selftest
#
#  options: --style auto|dust|leaves|petals|snow|rain|fireflies|stars|fog|glow|sheen|rays
#           --intensity 0.3–1.6  --amount 0.3–2  --speed 0.5–2  --seconds 6–20  --fps 24|30|60
#           --size WxH (the screen; ≤ 3840×2160)  --gif / --no-gif  --gif-dir DIR  --force  --json
#           --prompt "TEXT" (what should move, in your words: "langsamer goldener Schnee, viel, kein Nebel")
#           --colour picture|gold|white|pink|blue|… or #rrggbb  --scale 0.5–2 (particle size)
#           --wind auto|left|right|none  --variant N (another take: new places, same settings)
#           --preview (small and short — to judge a style in seconds)
#
#  Seamless: every motion is periodic in the loop's length — a falling leaf
#  crosses its lane a whole number of times, a mote is born again where it
#  was born, a twinkle has a whole number of beats — so the last frame runs
#  into the first. No camera move in the loop: Velvet's own Ken Burns drift
#  moves the live picture exactly as it moves the still.
#  The video holds ONLY the effect, never the picture (fx.mp4, packed: the top
#  half is what it adds — light, and the colour of what it lays over the
#  picture, premultiplied — the bottom half how much it covers). The wallpaper
#  lays it over the untouched still with a shader (assets/shaders/livewall.frag):
#  every pixel of the picture stays the still's own, the strength can change
#  live, and the loop has no seam — a whole picture in the video shimmered once
#  per loop where the encoder started afresh (measured: 2.4–2.8× an ordinary
#  frame step, while the frames themselves had none).
#  HEVC 10-bit (smooth glows on black never band), BT.709 TV range, tagged,
#  one keyframe; H.264 when x265 is missing. Frames go from the workers
#  through shared memory straight into ffmpeg — nothing big touches the disk.
#  The GIF (live.gif) is the picture with its effect, made in a second, small pass.
#  Polite: nice 19 + idle I/O; a game that takes the screen for more than a
#  few seconds stops the work (its memory too) — the queue starts it again later.
